{
  "moduleId": "m00191",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00191:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Alkylation and Acylation of Aromatic Rings: The Friedel–Crafts Reaction"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "5f16f284-48f8-4fe1-bce1-c04c80a6dda6"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00191:caption:fig-00001-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "A three carbon chain with a chlorine on C 2 reacts with aluminum trichloride to generate a three carbon chain carbocation with the positive charge on C 2. The carbocation reacts with benzene to form benzene bonded to isopropyl group."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "Aryl halide has a benzene ring bonded to a chlorine atom. Vinylic halide has an ethene group with a chlorine atom attached to one of the carbons."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00003",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00003"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00003",
      "class": "scaled-down",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00191:caption:fig-00003-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00003-alt",
          "text": "Benzene bonded to Y and R single bonded to X in the presence of aluminum trichloride do not react with one another. Possible Y groups are mentioned."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_003.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00004",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00004"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00004",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00004-alt",
          "text": "In the presence of aluminum trichloride, benzene reacts with 2-chloro-2-methylpropane. Monosubstituted and disubstituted products are formed, along with unreacted benzene."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_004.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00005",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00005"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00006",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00006"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00005",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00005-alt",
          "text": "The reactions of benzene with 1-chlorobutane and 1-chloro-2,2-dimethylpropane in the presence of aluminum trichloride are shown. Both generate products that result from a carbocation rearrangement, the mechanism of which is shown."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_005.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00007",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00007"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00006",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00006-alt",
          "text": "Benzene reacts with acetyl chloride in the presence of aluminum trichloride at 80 degrees Celsius to form acetophenone (95 percent)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_006.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00008",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00008"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00007",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00191:caption:fig-00007-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00007-alt",
          "text": "Three-step reaction shows an acid chloride reacting with aluminum trichloride to generate an acyl ion which then reacts with benzene to form a product with the acyl group attached to the benzene ring. Ball-and-stick model in electrostatic potential map of an acyl cation is depicted."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_007.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00009",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00009"
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00010",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00010"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00008",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00008-alt",
          "text": "The first reaction shows an alkyl chloride, r single bonded to C l) forms R plus and an A l C l 4 anion. The second reaction shows an organodiphosphate with an R group forming R plus and a diphosphate anion."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_008.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00011",
      "segmentId": "m00191:para:para-00011"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00009",
      "caption": {
        "segmentId": "m00191:caption:fig-00009-caption"
      },
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00191:alt:fig-00009-alt",
          "text": "Phytyl diphosphate forms phytyl carbocation, which reacts with 1,4-dihydroxynaphthoic acid to form phylloquinone (vitamin K 1)."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_16_03_009.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
